{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 6, :comments ["<b>And she opened it and saw him.</b> She saw that he was good and very beautiful." "<b>The child, and behold, a na'ar weeping.</b> She saw that though he was a yeled he was a na'ar weeping. For a child is called יֶלֶד from the time of birth until he is complete in growth, as in הַיְלָדִים אֲשֶׁר גָּדְלוּ אִתּוֹ (I Kings 12:10), said of Rehoboam, and likewise יְלָדִים אֲשֶׁר אֵין בָּהֶם כָּל מוּם (Daniel 1:4), said of Daniel and his companions. And from the time he stirs and moves with movements that are not yet perfect in order to attain an end, he is called נַעַר. Hence every attendant is called נַעַר, for indeed most of his movements are imperfect, as required to attain the end directed by his master. Thus the verse says she saw that while he was a yeled, newly born, behold he was a na'ar weeping — stirring with wondrous movement like one his age." "<b>And she had compassion on him.</b> That a child so handsome and prepared for such perfection should be cast into the river." "<b>And she said, From the children of the Hebrews is this one.</b> This is not a foundling born of harlotry, so as to be disposed to treachery, as it says: יִלְדֵי פֶשַׁע זֶרַע שָׁקֶר (Isaiah 57:4)."], :book :exodus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 2, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:23:59.811005755Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}